---
title: Tools
id: tools
order: 1
description: "Define isomorphic AI tools in TanStack AI with toolDefinition() for type-safe server- and client-side function calling across any framework."
keywords:
  - tanstack ai
  - tools
  - function calling
  - toolDefinition
  - isomorphic tools
  - server tools
  - client tools
  - type safety
---
NIFT_TANSTACK_DOCUMENT_V1
{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Tools (also called \"function calling\") allow AI models to interact with external systems, APIs, or perform computations. TanStack AI provides an isomorphic tool system that enables type-safe, framework-agnostic tool definitions that work on both server and client."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Tools enable your AI application to:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Fetch data"}]},{"type":"text","value":" from APIs or databases"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Perform calculations"}]},{"type":"text","value":" or data transformations"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Interact with services"}]},{"type":"text","value":" like email, calendars, or payment systems"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Execute client-side operations"}]},{"type":"text","value":" like updating UI or local storage"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Create hybrid tools"}]},{"type":"text","value":" that execute in both server and client contexts"}]}]}]},{"type":"blockquote","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Looking for provider-native tools like Anthropic web search, OpenAI code\ninterpreter, or Gemini URL context? See "},{"type":"link","href":"./provider-tools.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Provider Tools"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"framework-support","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Framework Support"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack AI works with "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"any"}]},{"type":"text","value":" JavaScript framework:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack Start, Next.js, Express, Remix, Fastify, etc."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"React, Vue, Solid, Svelte, vanilla JS, etc."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack AI works with any JavaScript framework."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"isomorphic-tool-architecture","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Isomorphic Tool Architecture"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack AI uses a two-step tool definition process:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Define once"}]},{"type":"text","value":" with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"toolDefinition()"},{"type":"text","value":" - Creates a shared tool schema"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Implement"}]},{"type":"text","value":" with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":".server()"},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":".client()"},{"type":"text","value":" - Add execution logic for each environment"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This approach provides:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Type Safety"}]},{"type":"text","value":": Full TypeScript inference from Zod schemas"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Code Reuse"}]},{"type":"text","value":": Define schemas once, use everywhere"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Flexibility"}]},{"type":"text","value":": Tools can execute on server, client, or both"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Schema Options"}]},{"type":"text","value":": Use Zod schemas or raw JSON Schema objects"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"schema-options","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Schema Options"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack AI supports two ways to define tool schemas:"}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"option-1-zod-schemas-recommended","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Option 1: Zod Schemas (Recommended)"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Zod schemas provide full TypeScript type inference and runtime validation:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { z } from \"zod\";\n\nconst inputSchema = z.object({\n  location: z.string().meta({ description: \"City name\" }),\n  unit: z.enum([\"celsius\", \"fahrenheit\"]).optional(),\n});","lang":"typescript"},{"type":"blockquote","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Note:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" For OpenAI-compatible providers, an omitted "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":".optional()"},{"type":"text","value":" tool field is absent when your tool runs. A "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":".nullable()"},{"type":"text","value":" field keeps "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"null"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"option-2-json-schema-objects","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Option 2: JSON Schema Objects"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For cases where you already have JSON Schema definitions or prefer not to use Zod, you can pass raw JSON Schema objects directly:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import type { JSONSchema } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\n\nconst inputSchema: JSONSchema = {\n  type: \"object\",\n  properties: {\n    location: {\n      type: \"string\",\n      description: \"City name\",\n    },\n    unit: {\n      type: \"string\",\n      enum: [\"celsius\", \"fahrenheit\"],\n    },\n  },\n  required: [\"location\"],\n};","lang":"typescript"},{"type":"blockquote","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Note:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" When using JSON Schema, TypeScript infers "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"unknown"},{"type":"text","value":" for input/output types (it cannot derive types from a JSON Schema at compile time), so you must narrow or cast "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"args"},{"type":"text","value":" before use. Zod schemas are recommended for full type safety."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Tip:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" Type safety from Zod schemas extends beyond tool execution. When you pass "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":".client()"},{"type":"text","value":" tools to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useChat"},{"type":"text","value":", a check on "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"part.name"},{"type":"text","value":" narrows "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"part.input"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"part.output"},{"type":"text","value":". See "},{"type":"link","href":"../chat/stream-events#type-safe-tool-call-events","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Type-safe tool call events"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"tool-definition","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Tool Definition"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Tools are defined using "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"toolDefinition()"},{"type":"text","value":" from "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/ai"},{"type":"text","value":":"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { toolDefinition } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\nimport { z } from \"zod\";\n\n// Step 1: Define the tool schema\nconst getWeatherDef = toolDefinition({\n  name: \"get_weather\",\n  description: \"Get the current weather for a location\",\n  inputSchema: z.object({\n    location: z.string().meta({ description: \"The city and state, e.g. San Francisco, CA\" }),\n    unit: z.enum([\"celsius\", \"fahrenheit\"]).optional(),\n  }),\n  outputSchema: z.object({\n    temperature: z.number(),\n    conditions: z.string(),\n    location: z.string(),\n  }),\n});\n\n// Step 2: Create a server implementation\nconst getWeatherServer = getWeatherDef.server(async ({ location, unit }) => {\n  const response = await fetch(\n    `https://api.weather.com/v1/current?location=${location}&unit=${\n      unit || \"fahrenheit\"\n    }`\n  );\n  const data = await response.json();\n  return {\n    temperature: data.temperature,\n    conditions: data.conditions,\n    location: data.location,\n  };\n});","lang":"typescript"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"using-json-schema","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Using JSON Schema"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If you prefer JSON Schema or have existing schema definitions:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { toolDefinition } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\nimport type { JSONSchema } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\n\n// Define schemas using JSON Schema\nconst inputSchema: JSONSchema = {\n  type: \"object\",\n  properties: {\n    location: {\n      type: \"string\",\n      description: \"The city and state, e.g. San Francisco, CA\",\n    },\n    unit: {\n      type: \"string\",\n      enum: [\"celsius\", \"fahrenheit\"],\n    },\n  },\n  required: [\"location\"],\n};\n\nconst outputSchema: JSONSchema = {\n  type: \"object\",\n  properties: {\n    temperature: { type: \"number\" },\n    conditions: { type: \"string\" },\n    location: { type: \"string\" },\n  },\n  required: [\"temperature\", \"conditions\", \"location\"],\n};\n\n// Create the tool definition\nconst getWeatherDef = toolDefinition({\n  name: \"get_weather\",\n  description: \"Get the current weather for a location\",\n  inputSchema,\n  outputSchema,\n});\n\n// With a raw JSON Schema, `args` is `unknown` — narrow it before use\n// (prefer a Zod schema for automatic typing).\nconst getWeatherServer = getWeatherDef.server(async (args) => {\n  if (typeof args !== \"object\" || args === null || !(\"location\" in args)) {\n    throw new Error(\"Invalid input: expected a location\");\n  }\n  const location = String(args.location);\n  const unit = \"unit\" in args ? String(args.unit) : \"fahrenheit\";\n  const response = await fetch(\n    `https://api.weather.com/v1/current?location=${location}&unit=${unit}`\n  );\n  return await response.json();\n});","lang":"typescript","meta":"group=json-schema-tools"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"using-tools-in-chat","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Using Tools in Chat"}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"server-side","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Server-Side"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { chat, toServerSentEventsResponse, toolDefinition } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\nimport { openaiText } from \"@tanstack/ai-openai\";\nimport { z } from \"zod\";\n\nconst getWeatherDef = toolDefinition({\n  name: \"get_weather\",\n  description: \"Get the current weather for a location\",\n  inputSchema: z.object({\n    location: z.string().meta({ description: \"The city and state, e.g. San Francisco, CA\" }),\n    unit: z.enum([\"celsius\", \"fahrenheit\"]).optional(),\n  }),\n  outputSchema: z.object({\n    temperature: z.number(),\n    conditions: z.string(),\n    location: z.string(),\n  }),\n});\n\nexport async function POST(request: Request) {\n  const { messages } = await request.json();\n\n  // Create server implementation\n  const getWeather = getWeatherDef.server(async ({ location, unit }) => {\n    const response = await fetch(`https://api.weather.com/v1/current?...`);\n    return await response.json();\n  });\n\n  const stream = chat({\n    adapter: openaiText(\"gpt-5.5\"),\n    messages,\n    tools: [getWeather], // Pass server tools\n  });\n\n  return toServerSentEventsResponse(stream);\n}","lang":"typescript"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"client-side-with-type-safety","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Client-Side with Type Safety"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { useChat, fetchServerSentEvents } from \"@tanstack/ai-react\";\nimport { \n  createChatClientOptions, \n  type InferChatMessages \n} from \"@tanstack/ai-client\";\nimport { toolDefinition } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\nimport { z } from \"zod\";\n\nconst updateUIDef = toolDefinition({\n  name: \"updateUI\",\n  description: \"Update the UI with a notification message\",\n  inputSchema: z.object({ message: z.string() }),\n  outputSchema: z.object({ success: z.boolean() }),\n});\n\nconst saveToStorageDef = toolDefinition({\n  name: \"saveToStorage\",\n  description: \"Save data to storage\",\n  inputSchema: z.object({ key: z.string(), value: z.string() }),\n  outputSchema: z.object({ saved: z.boolean() }),\n});\n\n// Create client implementations\nconst updateUI = updateUIDef.client((input) => {\n  // Update UI state\n  console.log(input.message);\n  return { success: true };\n});\n\nconst saveToStorage = saveToStorageDef.client((input) => {\n  localStorage.setItem(input.key, input.value);\n  return { saved: true };\n});\n\n// Create typed tools array (no 'as const' needed!)\nconst tools = [updateUI, saveToStorage];\n\nconst textOptions = createChatClientOptions({\n  connection: fetchServerSentEvents(\"/api/chat\"),\n  tools,\n});\n\n// Infer message types for full type safety\ntype ChatMessages = InferChatMessages<typeof textOptions>;\n\nfunction ChatComponent() {\n  const { messages } = useChat(textOptions);\n  \n  // messages is now fully typed with tool names and outputs!\n  return (\n    <div>\n      {messages.map((m) => (\n        <div key={m.id}>{m.role}</div>\n      ))}\n    </div>\n  );\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"hybrid-tools","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Hybrid Tools"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Tools can be implemented for both server and client, enabling flexible execution patterns:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { toolDefinition, chat } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\nimport { openaiText } from \"@tanstack/ai-openai\";\nimport { z } from \"zod\";\nimport { db } from \"./db\";\n\n// Define once\nconst addToCartDef = toolDefinition({\n  name: \"add_to_cart\",\n  description: \"Add item to shopping cart\",\n  inputSchema: z.object({\n    itemId: z.string(),\n    quantity: z.number(),\n  }),\n  outputSchema: z.object({\n    success: z.boolean(),\n    cartId: z.string(),\n  }),\n  needsApproval: true,\n});\n\n// Server implementation - Store in database\nconst addToCartServer = addToCartDef.server(async (input) => {\n  const cart = await db.carts.create({\n    data: { itemId: input.itemId, quantity: input.quantity },\n  });\n  return { success: true, cartId: cart.id };\n});\n\n// Client implementation - Update local wishlist\nconst addToCartClient = addToCartDef.client((input) => {\n  const wishlist = JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem(\"wishlist\") || \"[]\");\n  wishlist.push(input.itemId);\n  localStorage.setItem(\"wishlist\", JSON.stringify(wishlist));\n  return { success: true, cartId: \"local\" };\n});","lang":"typescript","meta":"group=tools"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"On the server, pass either the definition (for client execution) or the server implementation — in separate "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"chat()"},{"type":"text","value":" calls:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const messages = [{ role: 'user' as const, content: 'Add item abc to my cart' }]\n\n// Pass the definition: the client will execute the tool\nchat({\n  adapter: openaiText(\"gpt-5.5\"),\n  messages,\n  tools: [addToCartDef],\n});\n\n// Or pass the server implementation: the server will execute the tool\nchat({\n  adapter: openaiText(\"gpt-5.5\"),\n  messages,\n  tools: [addToCartServer],\n});","lang":"typescript","meta":"group=tools"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"type-safety-benefits","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Type Safety Benefits"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The isomorphic architecture provides complete type safety:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { useChat } from \"@tanstack/ai-react\";\nimport { fetchServerSentEvents } from \"@tanstack/ai-client\";\n\nfunction CartChat() {\n  const { messages: uiMessages } = useChat({\n    connection: fetchServerSentEvents(\"/api/chat\"),\n  });\n\n  // In your React component\n  uiMessages.forEach((message) => {\n    message.parts.forEach((part) => {\n      if (part.type === 'tool-call' && part.name === 'add_to_cart') {\n        // ✅ TypeScript knows part.name is literally 'add_to_cart'\n        // ✅ part.input is typed as { itemId: string, quantity: number }\n        // ✅ part.output is typed as { success: boolean, cartId: string } | undefined\n        \n        if (part.output) {\n          console.log(part.output.cartId); // ✅ Fully typed!\n        }\n      }\n    });\n  });\n\n  return null;\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"tool-execution-flow","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Tool Execution Flow"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Model decides to call a tool"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - Based on user input and tool descriptions"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Tool is identified"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - Server or client implementation"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Tool executes"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - Automatically on server or client"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Result is returned"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - To the model as a tool result message"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Model continues"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - Uses the result to generate a response"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"run-tools-one-at-a-time","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Run tools one at a time"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When the model calls several server tools in one turn, they start at the same time, and the model gets the results in the order of its calls. Some tools must not overlap, for example a tool that writes a file and a tool that runs the tests."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Set "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"toolExecution: 'sequential'"},{"type":"text","value":" on "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"chat()"},{"type":"text","value":" to run the tools of each turn one at a time, in call order:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { chat, toolDefinition } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { openaiText } from '@tanstack/ai-openai'\nimport { z } from 'zod'\n\nconst writeFile = toolDefinition({\n  name: 'write_file',\n  description: 'Write a file',\n  inputSchema: z.object({ path: z.string(), content: z.string() }),\n}).server(async ({ path }) => ({ written: path }))\n\nconst runTests = toolDefinition({\n  name: 'run_tests',\n  description: 'Run the test suite',\n  inputSchema: z.object({}),\n}).server(async () => ({ passed: true }))\n\nconst stream = chat({\n  adapter: openaiText('gpt-6.1-sol'),\n  messages: [{ role: 'user', content: 'Fix the bug, then run the tests.' }],\n  tools: [writeFile, runTests],\n  toolExecution: 'sequential',\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Client tools do not change: the client runs them as their calls arrive."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"progress-events-and-runtime-context","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Progress Events and Runtime Context"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A server tool's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":".server()"},{"type":"text","value":" implementation receives a second argument, the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ToolExecutionContext"},{"type":"text","value":" — "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"{ context, toolCallId, emitCustomEvent }"},{"type":"text","value":". Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"emitCustomEvent"},{"type":"text","value":" to stream typed progress to the client while the tool runs, and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"context"},{"type":"text","value":" to read request-scoped dependencies (auth, DB clients, etc.):"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { toolDefinition } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\nimport { z } from \"zod\";\n\ntype ImportContext = {\n  db: {\n    read(source: string): Promise<unknown[]>;\n    write(rows: unknown[]): Promise<void>;\n  };\n};\n\nconst importDataDef = toolDefinition({\n  name: \"import_data\",\n  description: \"Import data from a source\",\n  inputSchema: z.object({ source: z.string() }),\n  outputSchema: z.object({ imported: z.number() }),\n});\n\nconst importData = importDataDef.server<ImportContext>(async (input, { context, emitCustomEvent }) => {\n  emitCustomEvent(\"progress\", { step: 1, total: 3 });\n  const rows = await context.db.read(input.source);\n\n  emitCustomEvent(\"progress\", { step: 2, total: 3 });\n  await context.db.write(rows);\n\n  emitCustomEvent(\"progress\", { step: 3, total: 3 });\n  return { imported: rows.length };\n});","lang":"typescript"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Each "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"emitCustomEvent"},{"type":"text","value":" call flushes through durability immediately, so the\nclient can show progress while the tool still runs. Pass "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"{ batch: true }"},{"type":"text","value":"\nonly for a high-volume stream. See "},{"type":"link","href":"../protocol/custom-events","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Custom Events"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"See "},{"type":"link","href":"./server-tools","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Server Tools"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for the full runtime-context pattern."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"tool-states","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Tool States"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Tools go through different states during execution:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"awaiting-input"},{"type":"text","value":" - Tool call received, waiting for arguments"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"input-streaming"},{"type":"text","value":" - Partial arguments being streamed"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"input-complete"},{"type":"text","value":" - All arguments received"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"approval-requested"},{"type":"text","value":" - Tool requires user approval (if "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"needsApproval: true"},{"type":"text","value":")"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"approval-responded"},{"type":"text","value":" - User has approved/denied"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Once arguments (and approval, if required) are in, the result appears as "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"part.output"},{"type":"text","value":" on the tool-call part and as a separate sibling "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tool-result"},{"type":"text","value":" part whose "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"state"},{"type":"text","value":" is "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"complete"},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"error"},{"type":"text","value":". See "},{"type":"link","href":"./tool-architecture","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Tool Architecture"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for the full state model."}]},{"type":"blockquote","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Tip:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" If your use case involves calling multiple tools with complex logic (filtering, aggregation, parallel calls), consider "},{"type":"link","href":"../code-mode/code-mode","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Code Mode"}]},{"type":"text","value":" — it lets the LLM write a TypeScript program that orchestrates tools in a single execution instead of one tool call at a time."}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"next-steps","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Next Steps"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"./server-tools","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Server Tools"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - Learn about server-side tool execution"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"./client-tools","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Client Tools"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - Learn about client-side tool execution"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"./tool-approval","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Tool Approval Flow"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - Implement approval workflows"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"./tool-architecture","children":[{"type":"text","value":"How Tools Work"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - Deep dive into the tool architecture"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"./mcp","children":[{"type":"text","value":"MCP Server Tools"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - Connect to external MCP servers for additional tools"}]}]}]}],"headings":[{"id":"framework-support","text":"Framework Support","level":2},{"id":"isomorphic-tool-architecture","text":"Isomorphic Tool Architecture","level":2},{"id":"schema-options","text":"Schema Options","level":2},{"id":"option-1-zod-schemas-recommended","text":"Option 1: Zod Schemas (Recommended)","level":3},{"id":"option-2-json-schema-objects","text":"Option 2: JSON Schema Objects","level":3},{"id":"tool-definition","text":"Tool Definition","level":2},{"id":"using-json-schema","text":"Using JSON Schema","level":3},{"id":"using-tools-in-chat","text":"Using Tools in Chat","level":2},{"id":"server-side","text":"Server-Side","level":3},{"id":"client-side-with-type-safety","text":"Client-Side with Type Safety","level":3},{"id":"hybrid-tools","text":"Hybrid Tools","level":2},{"id":"type-safety-benefits","text":"Type Safety Benefits","level":2},{"id":"tool-execution-flow","text":"Tool Execution Flow","level":2},{"id":"run-tools-one-at-a-time","text":"Run tools one at a time","level":2},{"id":"progress-events-and-runtime-context","text":"Progress Events and Runtime Context","level":2},{"id":"tool-states","text":"Tool States","level":2},{"id":"next-steps","text":"Next Steps","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\nTools (also called \"function calling\") allow AI models to interact with external systems, APIs, or perform computations. TanStack AI provides an isomorphic tool system that enables type-safe, framework-agnostic tool definitions that work on both server and client.\n\nTools enable your AI application to:\n\n- **Fetch data** from APIs or databases\n- **Perform calculations** or data transformations\n- **Interact with services** like email, calendars, or payment systems\n- **Execute client-side operations** like updating UI or local storage\n- **Create hybrid tools** that execute in both server and client contexts\n\n> Looking for provider-native tools like Anthropic web search, OpenAI code\n> interpreter, or Gemini URL context? See [Provider Tools](./provider-tools.md).\n\n## Framework Support\n\nTanStack AI works with **any** JavaScript framework:\n- TanStack Start, Next.js, Express, Remix, Fastify, etc.\n- React, Vue, Solid, Svelte, vanilla JS, etc.\n\nTanStack AI works with any JavaScript framework.\n\n## Isomorphic Tool Architecture\n\nTanStack AI uses a two-step tool definition process:\n\n1. **Define once** with `toolDefinition()` - Creates a shared tool schema\n2. **Implement** with `.server()` or `.client()` - Add execution logic for each environment\n\nThis approach provides:\n\n- **Type Safety**: Full TypeScript inference from Zod schemas\n- **Code Reuse**: Define schemas once, use everywhere\n- **Flexibility**: Tools can execute on server, client, or both\n- **Schema Options**: Use Zod schemas or raw JSON Schema objects\n\n## Schema Options\n\nTanStack AI supports two ways to define tool schemas:\n\n### Option 1: Zod Schemas (Recommended)\n\nZod schemas provide full TypeScript type inference and runtime validation:\n\n```typescript\nimport { z } from \"zod\";\n\nconst inputSchema = z.object({\n  location: z.string().meta({ description: \"City name\" }),\n  unit: z.enum([\"celsius\", \"fahrenheit\"]).optional(),\n});\n```\n\n> **Note:** For OpenAI-compatible providers, an omitted `.optional()` tool field is absent when your tool runs. A `.nullable()` field keeps `null`.\n\n### Option 2: JSON Schema Objects\n\nFor cases where you already have JSON Schema definitions or prefer not to use Zod, you can pass raw JSON Schema objects directly:\n\n```typescript\nimport type { JSONSchema } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\n\nconst inputSchema: JSONSchema = {\n  type: \"object\",\n  properties: {\n    location: {\n      type: \"string\",\n      description: \"City name\",\n    },\n    unit: {\n      type: \"string\",\n      enum: [\"celsius\", \"fahrenheit\"],\n    },\n  },\n  required: [\"location\"],\n};\n```\n\n> **Note:** When using JSON Schema, TypeScript infers `unknown` for input/output types (it cannot derive types from a JSON Schema at compile time), so you must narrow or cast `args` before use. Zod schemas are recommended for full type safety.\n\n> **Tip:** Type safety from Zod schemas extends beyond tool execution. When you pass `.client()` tools to `useChat`, a check on `part.name` narrows `part.input` and `part.output`. See [Type-safe tool call events](../chat/stream-events#type-safe-tool-call-events).\n\n## Tool Definition\n\nTools are defined using `toolDefinition()` from `@tanstack/ai`:\n\n```typescript\nimport { toolDefinition } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\nimport { z } from \"zod\";\n\n// Step 1: Define the tool schema\nconst getWeatherDef = toolDefinition({\n  name: \"get_weather\",\n  description: \"Get the current weather for a location\",\n  inputSchema: z.object({\n    location: z.string().meta({ description: \"The city and state, e.g. San Francisco, CA\" }),\n    unit: z.enum([\"celsius\", \"fahrenheit\"]).optional(),\n  }),\n  outputSchema: z.object({\n    temperature: z.number(),\n    conditions: z.string(),\n    location: z.string(),\n  }),\n});\n\n// Step 2: Create a server implementation\nconst getWeatherServer = getWeatherDef.server(async ({ location, unit }) => {\n  const response = await fetch(\n    `https://api.weather.com/v1/current?location=${location}&unit=${\n      unit || \"fahrenheit\"\n    }`\n  );\n  const data = await response.json();\n  return {\n    temperature: data.temperature,\n    conditions: data.conditions,\n    location: data.location,\n  };\n});\n```\n\n### Using JSON Schema\n\nIf you prefer JSON Schema or have existing schema definitions:\n\n```typescript group=json-schema-tools\nimport { toolDefinition } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\nimport type { JSONSchema } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\n\n// Define schemas using JSON Schema\nconst inputSchema: JSONSchema = {\n  type: \"object\",\n  properties: {\n    location: {\n      type: \"string\",\n      description: \"The city and state, e.g. San Francisco, CA\",\n    },\n    unit: {\n      type: \"string\",\n      enum: [\"celsius\", \"fahrenheit\"],\n    },\n  },\n  required: [\"location\"],\n};\n\nconst outputSchema: JSONSchema = {\n  type: \"object\",\n  properties: {\n    temperature: { type: \"number\" },\n    conditions: { type: \"string\" },\n    location: { type: \"string\" },\n  },\n  required: [\"temperature\", \"conditions\", \"location\"],\n};\n\n// Create the tool definition\nconst getWeatherDef = toolDefinition({\n  name: \"get_weather\",\n  description: \"Get the current weather for a location\",\n  inputSchema,\n  outputSchema,\n});\n\n// With a raw JSON Schema, `args` is `unknown` — narrow it before use\n// (prefer a Zod schema for automatic typing).\nconst getWeatherServer = getWeatherDef.server(async (args) => {\n  if (typeof args !== \"object\" || args === null || !(\"location\" in args)) {\n    throw new Error(\"Invalid input: expected a location\");\n  }\n  const location = String(args.location);\n  const unit = \"unit\" in args ? String(args.unit) : \"fahrenheit\";\n  const response = await fetch(\n    `https://api.weather.com/v1/current?location=${location}&unit=${unit}`\n  );\n  return await response.json();\n});\n```\n\n## Using Tools in Chat\n\n### Server-Side\n\n```typescript\nimport { chat, toServerSentEventsResponse, toolDefinition } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\nimport { openaiText } from \"@tanstack/ai-openai\";\nimport { z } from \"zod\";\n\nconst getWeatherDef = toolDefinition({\n  name: \"get_weather\",\n  description: \"Get the current weather for a location\",\n  inputSchema: z.object({\n    location: z.string().meta({ description: \"The city and state, e.g. San Francisco, CA\" }),\n    unit: z.enum([\"celsius\", \"fahrenheit\"]).optional(),\n  }),\n  outputSchema: z.object({\n    temperature: z.number(),\n    conditions: z.string(),\n    location: z.string(),\n  }),\n});\n\nexport async function POST(request: Request) {\n  const { messages } = await request.json();\n\n  // Create server implementation\n  const getWeather = getWeatherDef.server(async ({ location, unit }) => {\n    const response = await fetch(`https://api.weather.com/v1/current?...`);\n    return await response.json();\n  });\n\n  const stream = chat({\n    adapter: openaiText(\"gpt-5.5\"),\n    messages,\n    tools: [getWeather], // Pass server tools\n  });\n\n  return toServerSentEventsResponse(stream);\n}\n```\n\n### Client-Side with Type Safety\n\n```tsx\nimport { useChat, fetchServerSentEvents } from \"@tanstack/ai-react\";\nimport { \n  createChatClientOptions, \n  type InferChatMessages \n} from \"@tanstack/ai-client\";\nimport { toolDefinition } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\nimport { z } from \"zod\";\n\nconst updateUIDef = toolDefinition({\n  name: \"updateUI\",\n  description: \"Update the UI with a notification message\",\n  inputSchema: z.object({ message: z.string() }),\n  outputSchema: z.object({ success: z.boolean() }),\n});\n\nconst saveToStorageDef = toolDefinition({\n  name: \"saveToStorage\",\n  description: \"Save data to storage\",\n  inputSchema: z.object({ key: z.string(), value: z.string() }),\n  outputSchema: z.object({ saved: z.boolean() }),\n});\n\n// Create client implementations\nconst updateUI = updateUIDef.client((input) => {\n  // Update UI state\n  console.log(input.message);\n  return { success: true };\n});\n\nconst saveToStorage = saveToStorageDef.client((input) => {\n  localStorage.setItem(input.key, input.value);\n  return { saved: true };\n});\n\n// Create typed tools array (no 'as const' needed!)\nconst tools = [updateUI, saveToStorage];\n\nconst textOptions = createChatClientOptions({\n  connection: fetchServerSentEvents(\"/api/chat\"),\n  tools,\n});\n\n// Infer message types for full type safety\ntype ChatMessages = InferChatMessages<typeof textOptions>;\n\nfunction ChatComponent() {\n  const { messages } = useChat(textOptions);\n  \n  // messages is now fully typed with tool names and outputs!\n  return (\n    <div>\n      {messages.map((m) => (\n        <div key={m.id}>{m.role}</div>\n      ))}\n    </div>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Hybrid Tools\n\nTools can be implemented for both server and client, enabling flexible execution patterns:\n\n```typescript group=tools\nimport { toolDefinition, chat } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\nimport { openaiText } from \"@tanstack/ai-openai\";\nimport { z } from \"zod\";\nimport { db } from \"./db\";\n\n// Define once\nconst addToCartDef = toolDefinition({\n  name: \"add_to_cart\",\n  description: \"Add item to shopping cart\",\n  inputSchema: z.object({\n    itemId: z.string(),\n    quantity: z.number(),\n  }),\n  outputSchema: z.object({\n    success: z.boolean(),\n    cartId: z.string(),\n  }),\n  needsApproval: true,\n});\n\n// Server implementation - Store in database\nconst addToCartServer = addToCartDef.server(async (input) => {\n  const cart = await db.carts.create({\n    data: { itemId: input.itemId, quantity: input.quantity },\n  });\n  return { success: true, cartId: cart.id };\n});\n\n// Client implementation - Update local wishlist\nconst addToCartClient = addToCartDef.client((input) => {\n  const wishlist = JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem(\"wishlist\") || \"[]\");\n  wishlist.push(input.itemId);\n  localStorage.setItem(\"wishlist\", JSON.stringify(wishlist));\n  return { success: true, cartId: \"local\" };\n});\n```\n\nOn the server, pass either the definition (for client execution) or the server implementation — in separate `chat()` calls:\n\n```typescript group=tools\nconst messages = [{ role: 'user' as const, content: 'Add item abc to my cart' }]\n\n// Pass the definition: the client will execute the tool\nchat({\n  adapter: openaiText(\"gpt-5.5\"),\n  messages,\n  tools: [addToCartDef],\n});\n\n// Or pass the server implementation: the server will execute the tool\nchat({\n  adapter: openaiText(\"gpt-5.5\"),\n  messages,\n  tools: [addToCartServer],\n});\n```\n\n## Type Safety Benefits\n\nThe isomorphic architecture provides complete type safety:\n\n```tsx\nimport { useChat } from \"@tanstack/ai-react\";\nimport { fetchServerSentEvents } from \"@tanstack/ai-client\";\n\nfunction CartChat() {\n  const { messages: uiMessages } = useChat({\n    connection: fetchServerSentEvents(\"/api/chat\"),\n  });\n\n  // In your React component\n  uiMessages.forEach((message) => {\n    message.parts.forEach((part) => {\n      if (part.type === 'tool-call' && part.name === 'add_to_cart') {\n        // ✅ TypeScript knows part.name is literally 'add_to_cart'\n        // ✅ part.input is typed as { itemId: string, quantity: number }\n        // ✅ part.output is typed as { success: boolean, cartId: string } | undefined\n        \n        if (part.output) {\n          console.log(part.output.cartId); // ✅ Fully typed!\n        }\n      }\n    });\n  });\n\n  return null;\n}\n```\n\n## Tool Execution Flow\n\n1. **Model decides to call a tool** - Based on user input and tool descriptions\n2. **Tool is identified** - Server or client implementation\n3. **Tool executes** - Automatically on server or client\n4. **Result is returned** - To the model as a tool result message\n5. **Model continues** - Uses the result to generate a response\n\n## Run tools one at a time\n\nWhen the model calls several server tools in one turn, they start at the same time, and the model gets the results in the order of its calls. Some tools must not overlap, for example a tool that writes a file and a tool that runs the tests.\n\nSet `toolExecution: 'sequential'` on `chat()` to run the tools of each turn one at a time, in call order:\n\n```ts\nimport { chat, toolDefinition } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { openaiText } from '@tanstack/ai-openai'\nimport { z } from 'zod'\n\nconst writeFile = toolDefinition({\n  name: 'write_file',\n  description: 'Write a file',\n  inputSchema: z.object({ path: z.string(), content: z.string() }),\n}).server(async ({ path }) => ({ written: path }))\n\nconst runTests = toolDefinition({\n  name: 'run_tests',\n  description: 'Run the test suite',\n  inputSchema: z.object({}),\n}).server(async () => ({ passed: true }))\n\nconst stream = chat({\n  adapter: openaiText('gpt-6.1-sol'),\n  messages: [{ role: 'user', content: 'Fix the bug, then run the tests.' }],\n  tools: [writeFile, runTests],\n  toolExecution: 'sequential',\n})\n```\n\nClient tools do not change: the client runs them as their calls arrive.\n\n## Progress Events and Runtime Context\n\nA server tool's `.server()` implementation receives a second argument, the `ToolExecutionContext` — `{ context, toolCallId, emitCustomEvent }`. Use `emitCustomEvent` to stream typed progress to the client while the tool runs, and `context` to read request-scoped dependencies (auth, DB clients, etc.):\n\n```typescript\nimport { toolDefinition } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\nimport { z } from \"zod\";\n\ntype ImportContext = {\n  db: {\n    read(source: string): Promise<unknown[]>;\n    write(rows: unknown[]): Promise<void>;\n  };\n};\n\nconst importDataDef = toolDefinition({\n  name: \"import_data\",\n  description: \"Import data from a source\",\n  inputSchema: z.object({ source: z.string() }),\n  outputSchema: z.object({ imported: z.number() }),\n});\n\nconst importData = importDataDef.server<ImportContext>(async (input, { context, emitCustomEvent }) => {\n  emitCustomEvent(\"progress\", { step: 1, total: 3 });\n  const rows = await context.db.read(input.source);\n\n  emitCustomEvent(\"progress\", { step: 2, total: 3 });\n  await context.db.write(rows);\n\n  emitCustomEvent(\"progress\", { step: 3, total: 3 });\n  return { imported: rows.length };\n});\n```\n\nEach `emitCustomEvent` call flushes through durability immediately, so the\nclient can show progress while the tool still runs. Pass `{ batch: true }`\nonly for a high-volume stream. See [Custom Events](../protocol/custom-events).\n\nSee [Server Tools](./server-tools) for the full runtime-context pattern.\n\n## Tool States\n\nTools go through different states during execution:\n\n- `awaiting-input` - Tool call received, waiting for arguments\n- `input-streaming` - Partial arguments being streamed\n- `input-complete` - All arguments received\n- `approval-requested` - Tool requires user approval (if `needsApproval: true`)\n- `approval-responded` - User has approved/denied\n\nOnce arguments (and approval, if required) are in, the result appears as `part.output` on the tool-call part and as a separate sibling `tool-result` part whose `state` is `complete` or `error`. See [Tool Architecture](./tool-architecture) for the full state model.\n\n> **Tip:** If your use case involves calling multiple tools with complex logic (filtering, aggregation, parallel calls), consider [Code Mode](../code-mode/code-mode) — it lets the LLM write a TypeScript program that orchestrates tools in a single execution instead of one tool call at a time.\n\n## Next Steps\n\n- [Server Tools](./server-tools) - Learn about server-side tool execution\n- [Client Tools](./client-tools) - Learn about client-side tool execution\n- [Tool Approval Flow](./tool-approval) - Implement approval workflows\n- [How Tools Work](./tool-architecture) - Deep dive into the tool architecture\n- [MCP Server Tools](./mcp) - Connect to external MCP servers for additional tools\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\ntitle: Tools\nid: tools\norder: 1\ndescription: \"Define isomorphic AI tools in TanStack AI with toolDefinition() for type-safe server- and client-side function calling across any framework.\"\nkeywords:\n  - tanstack ai\n  - tools\n  - function calling\n  - toolDefinition\n  - isomorphic tools\n  - server tools\n  - client tools\n  - type safety\n---\n\nTools (also called \"function calling\") allow AI models to interact with external systems, APIs, or perform computations. TanStack AI provides an isomorphic tool system that enables type-safe, framework-agnostic tool definitions that work on both server and client.\n\nTools enable your AI application to:\n\n- **Fetch data** from APIs or databases\n- **Perform calculations** or data transformations\n- **Interact with services** like email, calendars, or payment systems\n- **Execute client-side operations** like updating UI or local storage\n- **Create hybrid tools** that execute in both server and client contexts\n\n> Looking for provider-native tools like Anthropic web search, OpenAI code\n> interpreter, or Gemini URL context? See [Provider Tools](./provider-tools.md).\n\n## Framework Support\n\nTanStack AI works with **any** JavaScript framework:\n- TanStack Start, Next.js, Express, Remix, Fastify, etc.\n- React, Vue, Solid, Svelte, vanilla JS, etc.\n\nTanStack AI works with any JavaScript framework.\n\n## Isomorphic Tool Architecture\n\nTanStack AI uses a two-step tool definition process:\n\n1. **Define once** with `toolDefinition()` - Creates a shared tool schema\n2. **Implement** with `.server()` or `.client()` - Add execution logic for each environment\n\nThis approach provides:\n\n- **Type Safety**: Full TypeScript inference from Zod schemas\n- **Code Reuse**: Define schemas once, use everywhere\n- **Flexibility**: Tools can execute on server, client, or both\n- **Schema Options**: Use Zod schemas or raw JSON Schema objects\n\n## Schema Options\n\nTanStack AI supports two ways to define tool schemas:\n\n### Option 1: Zod Schemas (Recommended)\n\nZod schemas provide full TypeScript type inference and runtime validation:\n\n```typescript\nimport { z } from \"zod\";\n\nconst inputSchema = z.object({\n  location: z.string().meta({ description: \"City name\" }),\n  unit: z.enum([\"celsius\", \"fahrenheit\"]).optional(),\n});\n```\n\n> **Note:** For OpenAI-compatible providers, an omitted `.optional()` tool field is absent when your tool runs. A `.nullable()` field keeps `null`.\n\n### Option 2: JSON Schema Objects\n\nFor cases where you already have JSON Schema definitions or prefer not to use Zod, you can pass raw JSON Schema objects directly:\n\n```typescript\nimport type { JSONSchema } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\n\nconst inputSchema: JSONSchema = {\n  type: \"object\",\n  properties: {\n    location: {\n      type: \"string\",\n      description: \"City name\",\n    },\n    unit: {\n      type: \"string\",\n      enum: [\"celsius\", \"fahrenheit\"],\n    },\n  },\n  required: [\"location\"],\n};\n```\n\n> **Note:** When using JSON Schema, TypeScript infers `unknown` for input/output types (it cannot derive types from a JSON Schema at compile time), so you must narrow or cast `args` before use. Zod schemas are recommended for full type safety.\n\n> **Tip:** Type safety from Zod schemas extends beyond tool execution. When you pass `.client()` tools to `useChat`, a check on `part.name` narrows `part.input` and `part.output`. See [Type-safe tool call events](../chat/stream-events#type-safe-tool-call-events).\n\n## Tool Definition\n\nTools are defined using `toolDefinition()` from `@tanstack/ai`:\n\n```typescript\nimport { toolDefinition } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\nimport { z } from \"zod\";\n\n// Step 1: Define the tool schema\nconst getWeatherDef = toolDefinition({\n  name: \"get_weather\",\n  description: \"Get the current weather for a location\",\n  inputSchema: z.object({\n    location: z.string().meta({ description: \"The city and state, e.g. San Francisco, CA\" }),\n    unit: z.enum([\"celsius\", \"fahrenheit\"]).optional(),\n  }),\n  outputSchema: z.object({\n    temperature: z.number(),\n    conditions: z.string(),\n    location: z.string(),\n  }),\n});\n\n// Step 2: Create a server implementation\nconst getWeatherServer = getWeatherDef.server(async ({ location, unit }) => {\n  const response = await fetch(\n    `https://api.weather.com/v1/current?location=${location}&unit=${\n      unit || \"fahrenheit\"\n    }`\n  );\n  const data = await response.json();\n  return {\n    temperature: data.temperature,\n    conditions: data.conditions,\n    location: data.location,\n  };\n});\n```\n\n### Using JSON Schema\n\nIf you prefer JSON Schema or have existing schema definitions:\n\n```typescript group=json-schema-tools\nimport { toolDefinition } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\nimport type { JSONSchema } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\n\n// Define schemas using JSON Schema\nconst inputSchema: JSONSchema = {\n  type: \"object\",\n  properties: {\n    location: {\n      type: \"string\",\n      description: \"The city and state, e.g. San Francisco, CA\",\n    },\n    unit: {\n      type: \"string\",\n      enum: [\"celsius\", \"fahrenheit\"],\n    },\n  },\n  required: [\"location\"],\n};\n\nconst outputSchema: JSONSchema = {\n  type: \"object\",\n  properties: {\n    temperature: { type: \"number\" },\n    conditions: { type: \"string\" },\n    location: { type: \"string\" },\n  },\n  required: [\"temperature\", \"conditions\", \"location\"],\n};\n\n// Create the tool definition\nconst getWeatherDef = toolDefinition({\n  name: \"get_weather\",\n  description: \"Get the current weather for a location\",\n  inputSchema,\n  outputSchema,\n});\n\n// With a raw JSON Schema, `args` is `unknown` — narrow it before use\n// (prefer a Zod schema for automatic typing).\nconst getWeatherServer = getWeatherDef.server(async (args) => {\n  if (typeof args !== \"object\" || args === null || !(\"location\" in args)) {\n    throw new Error(\"Invalid input: expected a location\");\n  }\n  const location = String(args.location);\n  const unit = \"unit\" in args ? String(args.unit) : \"fahrenheit\";\n  const response = await fetch(\n    `https://api.weather.com/v1/current?location=${location}&unit=${unit}`\n  );\n  return await response.json();\n});\n```\n\n## Using Tools in Chat\n\n### Server-Side\n\n```typescript\nimport { chat, toServerSentEventsResponse, toolDefinition } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\nimport { openaiText } from \"@tanstack/ai-openai\";\nimport { z } from \"zod\";\n\nconst getWeatherDef = toolDefinition({\n  name: \"get_weather\",\n  description: \"Get the current weather for a location\",\n  inputSchema: z.object({\n    location: z.string().meta({ description: \"The city and state, e.g. San Francisco, CA\" }),\n    unit: z.enum([\"celsius\", \"fahrenheit\"]).optional(),\n  }),\n  outputSchema: z.object({\n    temperature: z.number(),\n    conditions: z.string(),\n    location: z.string(),\n  }),\n});\n\nexport async function POST(request: Request) {\n  const { messages } = await request.json();\n\n  // Create server implementation\n  const getWeather = getWeatherDef.server(async ({ location, unit }) => {\n    const response = await fetch(`https://api.weather.com/v1/current?...`);\n    return await response.json();\n  });\n\n  const stream = chat({\n    adapter: openaiText(\"gpt-5.5\"),\n    messages,\n    tools: [getWeather], // Pass server tools\n  });\n\n  return toServerSentEventsResponse(stream);\n}\n```\n\n### Client-Side with Type Safety\n\n```tsx\nimport { useChat, fetchServerSentEvents } from \"@tanstack/ai-react\";\nimport { \n  createChatClientOptions, \n  type InferChatMessages \n} from \"@tanstack/ai-client\";\nimport { toolDefinition } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\nimport { z } from \"zod\";\n\nconst updateUIDef = toolDefinition({\n  name: \"updateUI\",\n  description: \"Update the UI with a notification message\",\n  inputSchema: z.object({ message: z.string() }),\n  outputSchema: z.object({ success: z.boolean() }),\n});\n\nconst saveToStorageDef = toolDefinition({\n  name: \"saveToStorage\",\n  description: \"Save data to storage\",\n  inputSchema: z.object({ key: z.string(), value: z.string() }),\n  outputSchema: z.object({ saved: z.boolean() }),\n});\n\n// Create client implementations\nconst updateUI = updateUIDef.client((input) => {\n  // Update UI state\n  console.log(input.message);\n  return { success: true };\n});\n\nconst saveToStorage = saveToStorageDef.client((input) => {\n  localStorage.setItem(input.key, input.value);\n  return { saved: true };\n});\n\n// Create typed tools array (no 'as const' needed!)\nconst tools = [updateUI, saveToStorage];\n\nconst textOptions = createChatClientOptions({\n  connection: fetchServerSentEvents(\"/api/chat\"),\n  tools,\n});\n\n// Infer message types for full type safety\ntype ChatMessages = InferChatMessages<typeof textOptions>;\n\nfunction ChatComponent() {\n  const { messages } = useChat(textOptions);\n  \n  // messages is now fully typed with tool names and outputs!\n  return (\n    <div>\n      {messages.map((m) => (\n        <div key={m.id}>{m.role}</div>\n      ))}\n    </div>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Hybrid Tools\n\nTools can be implemented for both server and client, enabling flexible execution patterns:\n\n```typescript group=tools\nimport { toolDefinition, chat } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\nimport { openaiText } from \"@tanstack/ai-openai\";\nimport { z } from \"zod\";\nimport { db } from \"./db\";\n\n// Define once\nconst addToCartDef = toolDefinition({\n  name: \"add_to_cart\",\n  description: \"Add item to shopping cart\",\n  inputSchema: z.object({\n    itemId: z.string(),\n    quantity: z.number(),\n  }),\n  outputSchema: z.object({\n    success: z.boolean(),\n    cartId: z.string(),\n  }),\n  needsApproval: true,\n});\n\n// Server implementation - Store in database\nconst addToCartServer = addToCartDef.server(async (input) => {\n  const cart = await db.carts.create({\n    data: { itemId: input.itemId, quantity: input.quantity },\n  });\n  return { success: true, cartId: cart.id };\n});\n\n// Client implementation - Update local wishlist\nconst addToCartClient = addToCartDef.client((input) => {\n  const wishlist = JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem(\"wishlist\") || \"[]\");\n  wishlist.push(input.itemId);\n  localStorage.setItem(\"wishlist\", JSON.stringify(wishlist));\n  return { success: true, cartId: \"local\" };\n});\n```\n\nOn the server, pass either the definition (for client execution) or the server implementation — in separate `chat()` calls:\n\n```typescript group=tools\nconst messages = [{ role: 'user' as const, content: 'Add item abc to my cart' }]\n\n// Pass the definition: the client will execute the tool\nchat({\n  adapter: openaiText(\"gpt-5.5\"),\n  messages,\n  tools: [addToCartDef],\n});\n\n// Or pass the server implementation: the server will execute the tool\nchat({\n  adapter: openaiText(\"gpt-5.5\"),\n  messages,\n  tools: [addToCartServer],\n});\n```\n\n## Type Safety Benefits\n\nThe isomorphic architecture provides complete type safety:\n\n```tsx\nimport { useChat } from \"@tanstack/ai-react\";\nimport { fetchServerSentEvents } from \"@tanstack/ai-client\";\n\nfunction CartChat() {\n  const { messages: uiMessages } = useChat({\n    connection: fetchServerSentEvents(\"/api/chat\"),\n  });\n\n  // In your React component\n  uiMessages.forEach((message) => {\n    message.parts.forEach((part) => {\n      if (part.type === 'tool-call' && part.name === 'add_to_cart') {\n        // ✅ TypeScript knows part.name is literally 'add_to_cart'\n        // ✅ part.input is typed as { itemId: string, quantity: number }\n        // ✅ part.output is typed as { success: boolean, cartId: string } | undefined\n        \n        if (part.output) {\n          console.log(part.output.cartId); // ✅ Fully typed!\n        }\n      }\n    });\n  });\n\n  return null;\n}\n```\n\n## Tool Execution Flow\n\n1. **Model decides to call a tool** - Based on user input and tool descriptions\n2. **Tool is identified** - Server or client implementation\n3. **Tool executes** - Automatically on server or client\n4. **Result is returned** - To the model as a tool result message\n5. **Model continues** - Uses the result to generate a response\n\n## Run tools one at a time\n\nWhen the model calls several server tools in one turn, they start at the same time, and the model gets the results in the order of its calls. Some tools must not overlap, for example a tool that writes a file and a tool that runs the tests.\n\nSet `toolExecution: 'sequential'` on `chat()` to run the tools of each turn one at a time, in call order:\n\n```ts\nimport { chat, toolDefinition } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { openaiText } from '@tanstack/ai-openai'\nimport { z } from 'zod'\n\nconst writeFile = toolDefinition({\n  name: 'write_file',\n  description: 'Write a file',\n  inputSchema: z.object({ path: z.string(), content: z.string() }),\n}).server(async ({ path }) => ({ written: path }))\n\nconst runTests = toolDefinition({\n  name: 'run_tests',\n  description: 'Run the test suite',\n  inputSchema: z.object({}),\n}).server(async () => ({ passed: true }))\n\nconst stream = chat({\n  adapter: openaiText('gpt-6.1-sol'),\n  messages: [{ role: 'user', content: 'Fix the bug, then run the tests.' }],\n  tools: [writeFile, runTests],\n  toolExecution: 'sequential',\n})\n```\n\nClient tools do not change: the client runs them as their calls arrive.\n\n## Progress Events and Runtime Context\n\nA server tool's `.server()` implementation receives a second argument, the `ToolExecutionContext` — `{ context, toolCallId, emitCustomEvent }`. Use `emitCustomEvent` to stream typed progress to the client while the tool runs, and `context` to read request-scoped dependencies (auth, DB clients, etc.):\n\n```typescript\nimport { toolDefinition } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\nimport { z } from \"zod\";\n\ntype ImportContext = {\n  db: {\n    read(source: string): Promise<unknown[]>;\n    write(rows: unknown[]): Promise<void>;\n  };\n};\n\nconst importDataDef = toolDefinition({\n  name: \"import_data\",\n  description: \"Import data from a source\",\n  inputSchema: z.object({ source: z.string() }),\n  outputSchema: z.object({ imported: z.number() }),\n});\n\nconst importData = importDataDef.server<ImportContext>(async (input, { context, emitCustomEvent }) => {\n  emitCustomEvent(\"progress\", { step: 1, total: 3 });\n  const rows = await context.db.read(input.source);\n\n  emitCustomEvent(\"progress\", { step: 2, total: 3 });\n  await context.db.write(rows);\n\n  emitCustomEvent(\"progress\", { step: 3, total: 3 });\n  return { imported: rows.length };\n});\n```\n\nEach `emitCustomEvent` call flushes through durability immediately, so the\nclient can show progress while the tool still runs. Pass `{ batch: true }`\nonly for a high-volume stream. See [Custom Events](../protocol/custom-events).\n\nSee [Server Tools](./server-tools) for the full runtime-context pattern.\n\n## Tool States\n\nTools go through different states during execution:\n\n- `awaiting-input` - Tool call received, waiting for arguments\n- `input-streaming` - Partial arguments being streamed\n- `input-complete` - All arguments received\n- `approval-requested` - Tool requires user approval (if `needsApproval: true`)\n- `approval-responded` - User has approved/denied\n\nOnce arguments (and approval, if required) are in, the result appears as `part.output` on the tool-call part and as a separate sibling `tool-result` part whose `state` is `complete` or `error`. See [Tool Architecture](./tool-architecture) for the full state model.\n\n> **Tip:** If your use case involves calling multiple tools with complex logic (filtering, aggregation, parallel calls), consider [Code Mode](../code-mode/code-mode) — it lets the LLM write a TypeScript program that orchestrates tools in a single execution instead of one tool call at a time.\n\n## Next Steps\n\n- [Server Tools](./server-tools) - Learn about server-side tool execution\n- [Client Tools](./client-tools) - Learn about client-side tool execution\n- [Tool Approval Flow](./tool-approval) - Implement approval workflows\n- [How Tools Work](./tool-architecture) - Deep dive into the tool architecture\n- [MCP Server Tools](./mcp) - Connect to external MCP servers for additional tools\n"}
